ata updates automatically where possible.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Monday. Leadership ru=
ns a 20-minute capacity review. Resolve collisions,=0Aapprove moved work, a=
ssign backup coverage.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Midweek. Escalate only items that become=
 blocked, exceed planned hours, or=0Acreate a new operating risk.=0A=0A=C2=
=B7 Month-end. Compare planned hours with actuals. Update the estimate, ret=
ire=0Aobsolete obligations, add new recurring work.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Quarter-end=
. Review total compliance capacity by function. Decide whether to=0Arebala=
nce work, automate evidence creation, change the control, or add partner=0A=
support.=0A=0ABuild guardrails=0A=0A=C2=B7 Link to evidence. Do not duplica=
te controlled documents in the calendar.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Keep human approval on=
 interpretations, attestations, and control conclusions.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Restri=
ct rows and evidence links by role. Compliance visibility should not=0Acrea=
te a new access problem.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Require a backup owner for every mater=
ial obligation.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Record actual hours. A capacity plan that never=
 learns from actuals becomes=0Aanother stale checklist.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Separat=
e the obligation from the task. One requirement may generate several=0Atask=
s across functions.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Track recurring interruptions as defects in=
 the operating design, not as proof=0Athat the team needs to work faster.=
=0A=0AWhat the operator gets every week=0A=0A=C2=B7 A visible compliance lo=
ad before it becomes urgent.=0A=0A=C2=B7 A realistic view of which function=
s are overcommitted.=0A=0A=C2=B7 A ranked list of stale evidence and blocke=
d owners.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Reserved time for work that was previously invisible.=
=0A=0A=C2=B7 Better estimates based on actual hours.=0A=0A=C2=B7 A record o=
f where interruption cost keeps repeating.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Fewer leadership dec=
isions made from inboxes and memory.=0A=0ATwo paths to build it=0A=0APath 1=
: Internal build=0A=0AStart with Microsoft Lists, Airtable, Smartsheet, or =
a shared spreadsheet.=0AImport the next 90 days of obligations. Add the req=
uired fields above. Link=0Aevery row to its source system. Ask each owner t=
o estimate hours for one cycle,=0Athen replace estimates with actuals. Publ=
ish a six-week view and review it for=0A20 minutes every Monday. Automate r=
eminders only after ownership, evidence=0Alocation, and escalation rules ar=
e stable.=0A=0APath 2: ERP or existing partner=0A=0AAsk the existing ERP, Q=
MS, IT, or compliance partner to add the planning layer=0Awithout creating =
a second system of record. The deliverable should include=0Asource-system l=
inks, owner and backup fields, recurring triggers,=0Aevidence-freshness rul=
es, planned versus actual hours, role-based access, and a=0Asix-week capaci=
ty dashboard. Require the partner to show how vendor renewals,=0Aaccess cha=
nges, training completion, corrective actions, and audit requests=0Aenter t=
he calendar automatically. The partner should leave the operating team=0Awi=
th a weekly review rhythm, not another dashboard nobody owns.=0A=0APut comp=
liance on the capacity calendar before the next request puts it there=0Afor=
 you.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0ATHE OPERATIONS BRIEF=0A=0ABy Bill Murphy =C2=A0=C2=
=B7=C2=A0 Powered by Colony Spark [https://www.colonyspark.com/]=0A=0AAlrea=
dy reserving capacity for compliance work, or watching it interrupt the=0Aw=
eek one request at a time=3F Hit reply, I read every one. Bill=0A=0AYou=E2=
=80=99re receiving this because you subscribed to The Operations Brief.=0AU=
nsubscribe [[[custom:unsubscribe]]]
